<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    Cast campaign, lever 1 `castarith`: a pointer plus a constant byte offset is
    printed as an integer round trip.  coreutils ls -O0 `file_or_link_mode`
    (sub_53e7) prints

        v1 = *(unsigned int *)((long)a0 + 0xac);

    two casts for one 4-byte read, where the same address is element 0x2b of an
    `unsigned int *`.  Root cause (P9, ActionSetCasts).  The address reaches the
    cast pass as a plain INT_ADD, because P5 can build no PTRSUB or PTRADD for a
    `void *` base: it has no field at 0xac and no element size.  The ported
    rules price an INT_ADD as integer arithmetic, so `getInputCast` converts the
    pointer to `long` on the way in and `castOutput` converts the sum back to a
    pointer on the way out.

    Stage / sub-stage: P9 "casts".  The P0 assertion `option castarith on|off`
    decides whether such a sum, when it is used as a `T *` and the offset is a
    whole number of `T`s, becomes pointer arithmetic on `(T *)base` instead.

    The fixture (SysV x86-64):

        rd32:  mov eax,[rdi+0xac]; ret           the witness shape
        rdneg: movsx eax,word [rdi-0x1a]; ret    a negative offset, index -0xd
        rdodd: mov eax,[rdi+0x6a]; ret           a control - 0x6a is not a whole
                                                 number of 4-byte elements, so
                                                 the integer form stays
        wr16:  mov [rdi+0x82],si; ret            a store, element 0x41
        pass:  ebx = [rdi+4]; take(rdi + 0x10)   a pointer that is passed on,
                                                 not dereferenced here
        rdbyte: movzx eax,byte [rdi+0x11];       a byte widened for a signed
                cmp esi,eax; setle al            compare, and a word widened
        rdidx:  movzx eax,word [rdi+0x48];       twice for an index: castimplied
                cdqe; shl rax,4                  must still see the loaded type
                                                 through the subscript

    Pass 1 (`option castarith off`) reproduces the bug; pass 2 is the default;
    pass 3 turns `arraynotation off`, which spells the passed pointer as
    `(uint4 *)a0 + 4` instead of `&((uint4 *)a0)[4]`.
-->
